sweet-wlan-rxpool (lowclk 2026-11-14, tracker L-13, research 156 sections 4-7): the Wi-Fi rx buffer pool is sized like Linux brcmfmac (max_rxbufpost from the firmware's shared RAM) and refilled in batches, one doorbell per batch.
Observed (hardware, tracker L-13): under a 140-180 Mbit/s TCP download the AP deauths us; every bench boot shows rx starvation counters (`rx starved polls`, `fewest empty rx buffers`), and the drop boots show firmware-tossed tx (status 3) just before the DOWN [obs docs/ALL-ON-TRACKER.md L-13].
Cause [read, not yet proven to be THE cause]: the pool was 16 buffers (SWDP_NSLOT), reposted one per rx completion with a doorbell each; 16 x 1514 B at 180 Mbit/s is about 1 ms of air time (research 156 s.4 item 1 [calc]). Linux posts max_rxbufpost (u16 at shared RAM +34, 0 -> 255) and refills in
batches of up to 32 when 32 below it, one write-index update + doorbell per batch [Linux v6.6 brcmfmac pcie.c:223,246,1644-1647, msgbuf.c:58,974-975,987-1006; Pixel bcmdhd bcmpcie.h:373-374 max_host_rxbufs, dhd_msgbuf.c:5705-5724].
Changed: (1) sweet_wlan_g2.h swg_e reads the u16 at shared +0x22 once, read-only (swg_rd16, the word at +0x20 the engine already reads around), a failed read or all ones is a GAP and the pool stays 16. (2) swl25_env picks the pool (swdp_rx_pick, host-tested): boot-arg sweetwlanrxpost=N (16..255) overrides, else the firmware value
(0 -> 255, > 255 -> 255, < 16 -> 16), cut to the ring (depth - 1) and to the rx buffers the DATA block holds; one log line with firmware value, ring-1 depth, block capacity, ceiling and chosen count, and a GAP line for every adjustment. sweetwlanrxpost=16 = today's pool. Absent boot-arg = the new behaviour.
(3) sweet_wlan.c sweet_wlan_data_blk allocates the DATA block once for 255 rx buffers (or the boot-arg count) with the same allocator and checks (contig, S2MPU granule), GAP + the old 16-buffer block when that is refused. (4) wlan_en0.h refills the pool after the completions of a poll pass
(swdp_rx_refill_n: at least min(32, pool/4) buffers free, 1 for a pool of 16, at most 32 per batch, never past the ring end, never more than the ring has free) as ONE plan [ITEM x n, WRIDX, DB]: audited by swdp_audit (consecutive slots, one index = last slot + 1, no duplicate request id, only ring 1 may carry several items),
executed by swg_dp_exec (item words concatenated in a file-scope buffer), judged by the L25 whitelist (sweet_wlan_l25_wl.h: ring-1 item within 32 slots of the write index, index step 1..32 inside the ring, every published item re-read, lap count on the wrap; rx buffer cap rx_n from the glue's own block length).
(5) a 10 s line `en0 rx pool`: posted now of pool, fewest posted at a refill, most posted, batches, most buffers in one batch, buffers posted by batches, refills due but not done; the old `rx starved polls` / `fewest empty rx buffers` counters stay.
Also regenerated from shim/ (host-tested sources): 0174 (g2.h, g2d.h), 0199 (data.h, data_plan.h, en0.h), 0206 (l25.h, l25_wl.h, l25_glue.h), 0268 (en0_bus.h hunk). This patch carries the sweet_wlan.c part.
Host tests: shim/wlan_{data,en0,dp15,en0_live,l25_wl,glue_l25,g2}_host_test.c (the live soak runs the real audit, executor and judge with pools of 16, 48, 64, 72 and 255: at 255, 227 ring-1 doorbells for 7000 rx frames instead of 7000), tools/test_wlan_rxpool_mutants.py (93 mutants, all killed).
Not changed: no register, address, doorbell register or firmware command is new: the stores are the existing kinds (ring-1 item words, H2D write index slot, doorbell 0 with the ring-1 word); what is new is the SHAPE (several items before one index + doorbell) in the audit, the executor and the judge. NEW WRITE SHAPE, NOT YET REVIEWED (rule 8: the
driver decides on the review). The tx path, ring 0, flow ring, D2H consumes and the 16 rx posts of L25 are unchanged. No hardware run yet. ORDER: after 0356 (touches no wlan file; applies on the trunk stack too).
